n  i personally was super excited about  apple coming out with their own maxwell  pack  because of much faster charging than  regular qi charging  and this one is rated at 15 watts  compared to only five with anchors  and it can provide close to that if  you're using it  for pass-through wireless charging now  you actually get about 9.5 usable watts  of wireless charging to the iphone's  battery  which is slower than 12.5 with apple's  dedicated puck  but that's still much faster than  increased 5 watts of pass-through  charging  so your iphone will charge about twice  as fast if you're using it as a wireless  charging base  but unfortunately if you are using it on  the go without being plugged into the  wall  it will also only provide 5 watts  just like anchors which ruins the  biggest reason why i personally wanted  a genuine magsafe pack from apple now i  was still a bit  optimistic because apple's higher  voltage and efficient coil and circuitry  could mean that we might still get  faster speeds and better efficiency  so let's go ahead and see just how much  charge this thing can provide  compared to anker's cheaper solution i  tested with a practically new iphone 12  and started with it fully dead  apple's pack took three and a half  minutes to turn the phone on which  seemed good  but anchors did so faster at three  minutes after 15 minutes my iphone 12  reached 9  charge where anchors reached 10 percent  and after 30 minutes maxsafe was still  one percent behind at 18  compared to 19. skipping to the one hour  mark apple's pack  got us to a measly 36 percent and  anchors  still one percent ahead at 37. i have to  pause and say that these speeds are  really disappointing  if apple had actually allowed full max  save speeds the iphone 12 would reach  this amount in 35 minutes instead of an  hour  and with that it still seems like  apple's 5 watt charging  isn't any more efficient than anchors  after an hour and 30 minutes apples  reaches  53 and after 1 hour and 45 minutes  it's at 57 percent compared to anchors  59  showing that that one is still slightly  ahead and that is where another level of  disappointment came  because this is where apple's max safe  pack was completely  drained meaning your iphone 12 will only  get  57 extra charge from this 99  pack and it will take a long time to get  there and of course with the iphone 12  for max it will be  much lower than that anchor kept going  until it reached 87 percent  charge so we get an additional 30  capacity for an iphone 12 compared to  apple spec  and about 52 more usable overall  wireless charging capacity this shows us  that although magsafe might be more  efficient in the way the apple  doesn't have to use as large of a  battery cell because of the higher  voltage  the overall real-world charging  efficiency isn't that much better  and if you're only trying to reach 50  charge anchors could do that faster  and then at the same time it can charge  while you're doing intensive tests  with that how long do these take to  charge well apples is fairly quick  since it accepts 15 real world watts  through its lightning connector  compared to 9.5 and because the battery  is smaller  if you're only charging it by itself it  will complete in an hour and 15 minutes  compared to 3 hours and 15 minutes with  anchor so if you want to quickly charge  before going somewhere apple definitely  takes the win there's also a few other  differences that you have to know about  before choosing which one to buy first  off the mac safe pack uses a lightning  connector to charge  which can be more convenient if you  don't use usb type-c  but it doesn't come with a cable like  anchors does because of this the only  way you can charge  a device is by using wireless charging  not  wired and if you're not charging an  iphone 12  just like apple's 40 maxsafe puck in  regular qi charging mode it will only  output between 2.5 to 3.3 watts  so if you want to charge a friend's  older iphone or 